The MM crate is a new Apache-2.0 policy extraction, written against Linux
behaviour; its own `crates/linux/mm/NOTICE` records that the package still
quotes 59 verbatim Linux lines in 21 fenced blocks, and which other projects
were consulted for observable contracts. No reference tree for Zircon,
Asterinas, FreeBSD, gVisor, liburing, or libpcap is recorded in this repository,
so none of those consultations can be scanned here — see the last paragraph.

Excerpt policy for this directory, stated exactly
-------------------------------------------------

This workspace does reproduce Linux source text. Excerpts of Linux v7.2.3 C and
comments — GPL-2.0-only, (C) The Linux Kernel Authors — are quoted verbatim in
Rust comments as the observable-semantics authority. They are documentation
only: no Linux code is linked, assembled, or compiled here, and the Apache-2.0
grant in the root `NOTICE` does not extend to the quoted text.

The policy these excerpts are held to:

* Every excerpt is attributed to the Linux file it came from, at or above the
  block, and either by line range or by the enclosing Linux function
  (`mm/mmap.c:check_brk_limits()`).
* The inline marker line
  `Excerpt: Linux v7.2.3 <file>:<lines> — GPL-2.0-only, (C) The Linux Kernel
  Authors` is the required form for new excerpts. It is on every matched block in
  `net`, `mount`, `aio`, and `random` — 18 of the 49 blocks this directory counts
  at ≥ 40, and 20 of 59 at ≥ 25, from 21 marker lines (12 in `net`, 5 in `mount`,
  3 in `random`, 1 in `aio`; three of those markers open no counted block because
  their hunks are made of short lines). Of the 31 unmarked blocks, 1 is cited by
  surrounding prose with a correct Linux line range and 30 name only the file and
  the enclosing function. Every block, marked or not, is listed with its gap
  flagged in `docs/upstream-provenance.md`.
* Every package under `crates/linux/` that holds Linux source text carries a
  `NOTICE` inventorying its hunks with measured line counts.

Measured against the Linux 7.2.3 tree with the scan documented in
`docs/upstream-provenance.md` (whitespace-normalised lines of at least 40
characters, byte-identical to a Linux `*.c` or `*.h` line): 110 lines in 49
fenced blocks across 20 files inside `crates/linux/**`, 211 lines in 59 blocks
across 22 files at the inclusive ≥ 25-character threshold, plus 25 further
verbatim lines in 5 files that sit outside fenced doc blocks — in unfenced
`///` prose lists and in `//` comments (`io-uring` 17, `process` 4, `fsnotify` 3,
`mm` 1; 41 such lines at ≥ 25, 3 of which are Rust `code` rather than text).
Those outside-fence lines carry the same file-and-range citations but not the
`Excerpt:` marker, and are a code-side follow-up for their owners.

The scan was run against Linux only. No reference tree for Zircon, Asterinas,
FreeBSD, gVisor, liburing, or libpcap is recorded anywhere in this repository,
so the absence of text from those projects is unverified rather than measured;
the packages that name them as consulted sources state them as consulted for
semantics, not as sources of copied text.
